         reasoning='No excerpts yet. Need broad search for both systems and their library-learning mechanisms before comparing.'
  [search_all] 'Stitch library learning DreamCoder difference' -> 6 new excerpt(s)
  [search_all] 'Stitch system library learning vs DreamCoder library learning' -> 2 new excerpt(s)
  [search_all] 'Stitch DreamCoder library learning' -> 1 new excerpt(s)
  [plan] action=answer paper='' queries=[]
         reasoning='We have enough from the Stitch paper to answer, and the named system DreamCoder is covered by excerpts from the same paper. The key difference is: DreamCoder does iterative library learning by alternating between program synthesis and updating the library, using a neurally guided enumerative search and a probabilistic Bayesian objective. Stitch instead learns libraries with a top-down branch-and-bound search over abstractions, optimizing a compression/cost objective directly. Stitch is also presented as much more efficient (faster and lower memory), and unlike DreamCoder it can be used as an anytime algorithm. The paper also notes a functional limitation: Stitch cannot learn higher-order abstractions in the same way DreamCoder can in some domains, though it can recover them when layered on top of DreamCoder’s version spaces in a later experiment.'
  [answer] from 9 excerpt(s), 1 tool call(s)

The excerpts say Stitch differs from DreamCoder in both **how it searches** and **what it can learn**.

- **DreamCoder learns libraries iteratively**: “the system is initialized with a low-level DSL, and then alternates between synthesizing programs … and updating the library of abstractions” [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.18].
- **Stitch uses a top-down branch-and-bound search**: “Stitch synthesizes the maximally-compressive abstraction for a corpus of programs through a branch-and-bound top-down search, computing an upper bound on the compression of any partially-constructed abstraction to guide and prune the search” [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.28].
- In the comparison on library quality, the paper says Stitch “generally learns libraries of comparable and often greater quality than DreamCoder” [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.19].
- The excerpt also notes a limitation: “Stitch cannot learn higher-order abstractions, which are useful in this domain” [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.19].
- On efficiency, Stitch is much faster and uses less memory: it “takes tens of milliseconds” and achieves “a 3-4 order of magnitude speed-up over DreamCoder—while also requiring more than 2 orders of magnitude less memory” [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.20].

So, from the excerpts: DreamCoder is an **iterative deductive learner**, while Stitch is a **top-down, branch-and-bound compressor** that is typically much faster and more memory-efficient, though it cannot learn some higher-order abstractions [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23 p.18; p.19; p.20; p.28].

